    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        If you are buying one thing for knee pain, the evidence points to
        <strong>collagen peptides at 5–10g daily</strong>. If you are buying two, add
        <strong>vitamin D3</strong> — deficiency is close to universal in urban India and it
        undermines everything else you take. If you are buying three, add
        <strong>curcumin</strong>, which has repeatedly matched NSAIDs on osteoarthritis pain
        scores in head-to-head trials.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Strong-to-moderate evidence:</strong> collagen peptides, vitamin D3 (if deficient), curcumin, Boswellia serrata.</li>
        <li><strong>Mixed evidence:</strong> glucosamine sulphate, chondroitin, oral hyaluronic acid, omega-3.</li>
        <li><strong>Weak or insufficient evidence:</strong> MSM, Cissus quadrangularis, most proprietary "joint blends".</li>
        <li><strong>More effective than all of the above:</strong> quadriceps strengthening and weight management. No supplement outperforms them.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

