      <h2 id="worth-it">The three worth your money</h2>

      <h3>1. Collagen peptides — 5,000–10,000mg daily</h3>
      <p>
        The best-supported option, and the one with trials designed around function rather than
        laboratory markers. Clark and colleagues followed 147 athletes for 24 weeks on 10g of
        collagen hydrolysate daily and found <strong>significant reductions in joint pain when
        walking, standing, carrying and lifting</strong>. Zdzieblik and colleagues replicated the
        pattern at 5g daily over 12 weeks in young adults with functional knee pain.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mechanism: absorbed peptides appear to signal chondrocytes — cartilage cells — to increase
        matrix production, and they supply glycine and proline, the amino acids that dominate
        collagen's structure. Type I collagen also makes up around 90% of the organic matrix of
        bone, so the benefit is not limited to cartilage.
      </p>
      <p>
        The catch: <strong>dose</strong>. Most joint capsules contain a few hundred milligrams.
        Nothing below 2.5g has meaningful supporting evidence, and the joint trials clustered at
        5–10g. Read the full breakdown in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Vitamin D3 — 600–2,000 IU daily</h3>
      <p>
        Not glamorous, and the one I check first in clinic. Studies of urban Indian populations have
        repeatedly reported <strong>vitamin D deficiency in 70–90% of adults</strong> — a
        remarkable figure in a country with abundant sunlight, driven by indoor work, sun
        avoidance, pollution and skin pigmentation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Vitamin D governs how much dietary calcium you actually absorb. Deficiency causes diffuse
        musculoskeletal pain and proximal muscle weakness in its own right, and weak quadriceps is
        one of the strongest risk factors for knee osteoarthritis progression.
      </p>
      <p>
        Important nuance, stated plainly: supplementing vitamin D in people who are
        <strong>already replete</strong> does not improve osteoarthritis outcomes. This is a
        deficiency correction, not a treatment. Get a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level tested — it is an
        inexpensive test and it converts guesswork into a decision.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Curcumin — around 1,000mg daily of a bioavailable form</h3>
      <p>
        Curcumin inhibits NF-κB, a central regulator of the inflammatory cascade. A 2016
        meta-analysis in the <em>Journal of Medicinal Food</em> pooled randomised trials and found
        roughly 1,000mg per day <strong>improved osteoarthritis symptoms</strong>, with several
        head-to-head trials showing pain relief comparable to ibuprofen and a better
        gastrointestinal side-effect profile.
      </p>
      <p>
        The caveat is absorption. Plain turmeric powder is poorly bioavailable — you need a
        formulated version (piperine-enhanced, phospholipid-complexed or water-soluble). "Contains
        turmeric" on a label tells you very little.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mixed">The mixed cases</h2>

      <h3>Glucosamine sulphate — 1,500mg daily</h3>
      <p>
        The default joint supplement for three decades, and the evidence is genuinely
        contested. The <strong>GAIT trial</strong> (<em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 2006)
        randomised nearly 1,600 knee osteoarthritis patients and found glucosamine and chondroitin
        <strong>no better than placebo</strong> overall. A subgroup with moderate-to-severe pain
        benefited from the combination, but that result was exploratory and has not reliably
        replicated.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some meta-analyses of pharmaceutical-grade glucosamine sulphate still find a small benefit;
        several major clinical guidelines now decline to recommend it. My position: it is cheap,
        safe and worth a three-month trial at the full 1,500mg dose if you want to test it — but
        judge it honestly at twelve weeks and stop if nothing has changed.
      </p>

      <h3>Chondroitin sulphate — 800–1,200mg daily</h3>
      <p>
        Same trial history, same verdict, usually sold alongside glucosamine. Some evidence for
        slowing joint space narrowing on imaging; less convincing evidence that patients feel
        better. Quality control is also a genuine problem — independent testing has repeatedly
        found chondroitin products containing substantially less than the labelled amount.
      </p>

      <h3>Oral hyaluronic acid — 80–200mg daily</h3>
      <p>
        Hyaluronic acid is the molecule that makes synovial fluid viscous, and injected HA is an
        established treatment. The oral case is weaker but not absent: several small trials,
        including work by Oe and colleagues, reported improved knee pain scores over 8–12 weeks at
        doses around 200mg. Sensible as part of a stack; thin as a standalone purchase.
      </p>

      <h3>Omega-3 fatty acids — 1,000–3,000mg EPA/DHA</h3>
      <p>
        Strong evidence in <em>rheumatoid</em> arthritis, which is an autoimmune inflammatory
        disease. Much weaker in osteoarthritis, which is primarily mechanical. Worth taking for
        cardiovascular and general health; do not expect it to resolve a worn knee.
      </p>

      <h2 id="weak">The oversold ones</h2>

      <h3>MSM (methylsulfonylmethane)</h3>
      <p>
        Appears in almost every joint blend. The trials are small, short and mostly of low quality,
        with inconsistent results. It is not harmful and it is cheap, but it is present in most
        formulas because it is inexpensive filler with a scientific-sounding name, not because the
        data demands it.
      </p>

      <h3>Cissus quadrangularis</h3>
      <p>
        A traditional Ayurvedic preparation with a long history in fracture healing and some
        preliminary work on bone and tendon. I will be straightforward, including about our own
        formula: <strong>the modern clinical evidence base for knee osteoarthritis is thin.</strong>
        <a href="/products/skelecoll">Skelecoll</a> includes it for muscular endurance around the
        joint on the strength of traditional use and early data — not because it has been proven in
        the way collagen peptides have.
      </p>

      <h3>Proprietary "joint complex" blends</h3>
      <p>
        If a label lists a "proprietary blend" with a single total weight and no per-ingredient
        doses, assume the expensive actives are present in trace amounts and the cheap ones make up
        the mass. This is the most common way an underdosed product is made to look comprehensive.
        <strong>If you cannot see a milligram figure next to each active, do not buy it.</strong>
      </p>

