      <h2 id="not-supplements">What beats every supplement here</h2>

      <p>
        I would be doing you a disservice if I ended without this section. Two interventions
        outperform everything above, and both are free:
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Quadriceps strengthening.</strong> Quadriceps weakness is among the
        best-established modifiable risk factors for knee osteoarthritis pain and progression.
        Exercise therapy is a first-line recommendation in essentially every major clinical
        guideline for knee OA. No supplement has that standing.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Weight management.</strong> Loads across the knee are amplified several times over
        during walking and stair climbing, which is why modest weight loss produces
        disproportionate reductions in joint load and pain. If you are carrying excess weight, this
        is the single highest-yield change available to you.
      </p>

      <p>
        Supplements are a supporting layer on top of those two. Sequenced the other way round, they
        will disappoint you.
      </p>

      <h2 id="red-flags">Red flags that need a doctor</h2>

      <p>Stop shopping and get assessed if you have any of the following:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>A hot, red, swollen knee</strong> — possible infection or acute inflammatory arthritis; this is urgent</li>
        <li><strong>Locking, catching or giving way</strong> — typically mechanical, such as a meniscal tear</li>
        <li><strong>Night pain</strong> or pain at complete rest</li>
        <li><strong>Sudden severe pain after trauma</strong>, or inability to bear weight</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple swollen joints with morning stiffness beyond an hour</strong> — suggestive of inflammatory arthritis, which needs disease-modifying treatment, not supplements</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="jr-disclaimer">
        This article is for general information and is not medical advice. Nutritional supplements
        are not intended to diagnose, treat, cure or prevent any disease, including osteoarthritis.
        Dosages mentioned reflect those used in published research and are not personal
        recommendations. If you have persistent knee pain, a diagnosed joint condition, a fish or
        shellfish allergy, or take prescription medication, speak to a qualified healthcare
        professional before starting supplementation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the best supplement for knee pain?</summary>
          <p>
            On current evidence, collagen peptides at 5–10g daily have the most consistent
            randomised trial support for activity-related knee pain, followed by vitamin D3 where
            deficiency is present and bioavailable curcumin for inflammatory pain. No supplement
            outperforms quadriceps strengthening and weight management.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does glucosamine actually work for knees?</summary>
          <p>
            The evidence is genuinely mixed. The large independent GAIT trial found glucosamine no
            better than placebo overall for knee osteoarthritis, though a moderate-to-severe pain
            subgroup benefited from glucosamine combined with chondroitin. It is cheap and safe, so
            a three-month trial at the full 1,500mg dose is reasonable — but judge it honestly and
            stop if nothing changes.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long do knee pain supplements take to work?</summary>
          <p>
            Allow 12 weeks. Collagen trials measured significant pain reduction between weeks 8 and
            24, and curcumin trials typically ran 6 to 12 weeks. Anything promising relief in days
            is describing an anti-inflammatory effect, not tissue change.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take vitamin D for knee pain?</summary>
          <p>
            Only if you are deficient — but in urban India that is likely, with studies reporting
            deficiency in 70–90% of adults. Vitamin D deficiency itself causes musculoskeletal pain
            and muscle weakness. Supplementing when already replete does not improve osteoarthritis
            outcomes, so get a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level tested first.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is turmeric as good as ibuprofen for joint pain?</summary>
          <p>
            Several head-to-head trials found bioavailable curcumin at around 1,000mg daily
            produced pain relief comparable to ibuprofen in knee osteoarthritis, with fewer
            gastrointestinal side effects. Plain turmeric powder is poorly absorbed, so the
            formulation matters as much as the dose.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take collagen and glucosamine together?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes — there is no known interaction and they act on different parts of the joint.
            Collagen peptides support cartilage matrix production, while glucosamine and hyaluronic
            acid support the proteoglycan and lubrication side. Skelecoll combines both in a single
            daily shot.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

